(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-pd1-exam3-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"For program assignments, where do vital signs belong and how should they be presented?","choices":["Within the history of present illness, written as prose","At the very end of the physical examination section, recorded in imperial units","In the assessment, alongside the final diagnosis","As the first element of the physical examination, as a bulleted list in metric units"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that for these program assignments vital signs come first in the physical examination and are best documented as a bulleted list using metric units. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What supplies the medial quadrant of the breast vascularly?","choices":["Perforating branches (2,3,4) from the internal thoracic artery and branches of the anterior intercostal artery","The external mammary branch arising directly from the lateral thoracic artery laterally","Pectoral branches originating directly from the thoracoacromial arterial trunk superiorly","The axillary artery directly, with no other clinically significant named branches"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial quadrant is supplied by perforating branches of the internal thoracic artery and anterior intercostal artery branches. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which movement is tested as part of temporomandibular joint range of motion?","choices":["Shrugging the shoulders upward","Rotating the neck to look over the shoulder","Flexing the elbow to ninety degrees","Moving the jaw laterally to each side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Temporomandibular joint range of motion includes opening and closing the mouth, moving the jaw laterally to each side, and protruding and retracting the jaw. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which additional detail is part of an accurate musculoskeletal pain history?","choices":["The patient's favorite hobby","The color of the examination room","The examiner's own glove size","The duration of the complaint"],"answer":3,"exp":"An accurate musculoskeletal history includes the mechanism of injury, the location and nature of pain, a pain scale, the duration of the complaint, and exacerbating or relieving maneuvers. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the scope of a SOAP note compared with a history and physical?","choices":["More focused than a history and physical","Broader than a history and physical, since it adds the daily course","Identical in scope, differing only in the order of its sections","Longer than a history and physical, since it carries prior entries forward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the SOAP note is more focused than an H&P while still being a standardized way to document encounters. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Normal hip flexion, tested supine by bringing the knee to the chest, is approximately what range?","choices":["About 30 degrees","About 45 to 50 degrees","About 180 degrees","About 110 to 130 degrees"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal hip flexion, tested supine by bringing the knee toward the chest, is approximately 110 to 130 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which list matches the seven attributes of a symptom given in the lecture?","choices":["Onset, duration, severity, frequency, laterality, radiation, relief","Location, quality, quantity or severity, timing, onset, palliative or provocative factors, associated manifestations","Site, character, radiation, associations, timing, exacerbating and relieving factors, and overall severity","Position, pattern, precipitant, progression, pain score, prior episodes, plan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the seven attributes the lecture names for describing a symptom in the history of present illness. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the mons pubis?","choices":["Hair-covered fat pads overlying the symphysis pubis, with an inverted-triangle hair distribution","The area located between the vaginal introitus and the anus, bounded by the thighs","The thin, pink-red labial folds that extend anteriorly to form the clitoral prepuce","The thin membrane that partially covers the introitus in a virginal female"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mons pubis is a hair-covered fat pad over the symphysis pubis with an inverted-triangle hair pattern and a flat base extending onto the abdomen. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"In what order does the lecture say the physical examination should be documented?","choices":["Beginning with the system related to the chief complaint","In the order the findings happened to be discovered","Grouping all normal findings first, then all abnormal findings","Starting with the general survey and moving through all body systems"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks you to document what you observed, starting with the general survey and working through all body systems. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"In OLD ChARTS, what do the letters O and L stand for?","choices":["Onset and location","Origin and level","Onset and length","Occurrence and laterality"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. OLD ChARTS runs onset, location, duration, character, alleviating and aggravating factors, radiation, temporal pattern, and symptoms associated. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How does the lecture define the content of the objective section?","choices":["The patient's own account of how the presenting problem began","Observations, measurements, and tests performed during the encounter","The diagnoses you are now prepared to commit to in writing","The instructions given to the patient on leaving the encounter"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture subtitles this section “what you found” and defines it in exactly these terms. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How is the breast's glandular tissue organized?","choices":["In lobes around the nipple, with a duct draining each lobe","Ducts merge into a single duct before reaching the nipple","In lobes surrounding the axilla rather than the nipple region","In a single continuous sheet with no lobar organization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Glandular tissue is organized into lobes around the nipple, each drained by its own duct. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"A reflex graded 4 plus, very brisk with rhythmic oscillations, is described as having what?","choices":["Areflexia","Fasciculation","Clonus","Atrophy"],"answer":2,"exp":"A four plus reflex is very brisk and hyperactive with clonus — rhythmic oscillations between flexion and extension. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 52"},{"q":"What are Skene's glands?","choices":["Periurethral glands with openings on either side of the urethral meatus","The membrane covering the introitus in a virginal female","The erectile tissue and glans found at the tip of the clitoris","The greater vestibular glands located at 5 and 7 o'clock"],"answer":0,"exp":"Skene's glands are periurethral glands whose openings lie on either side of the urethral meatus. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which tuning fork is used for the Rinne and Weber hearing tests?","choices":["A 128 Hertz tuning fork","A 60 Hertz tuning fork","A 512 Hertz tuning fork","A 1000 Hertz tuning fork"],"answer":2,"exp":"A 512 Hertz tuning fork is used for the Rinne and Weber hearing tests, while a 128 Hertz fork tests vibratory sense. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"The lecture warns about directing the conversation too tightly. What is the stated risk?","choices":["The encounter will exceed the time allocated for the visit","The patient's story is affected, which can lead to omissions and errors","The clinician will be unable to complete the review of systems","The patient will refuse to answer any further questions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to direct the conversation but not control it in a way that affects the patient's story, because that can cause omissions and errors. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Having the patient say 'Ah' and observing the soft palate and uvula tests which cranial nerves?","choices":["The olfactory and optic nerves (one and two)","The trochlear and abducens nerves (four and six)","The glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves (nine and ten)","The accessory and hypoglossal nerves (eleven and twelve)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Saying Ah to observe symmetric rise of the soft palate and midline uvula, plus the gag reflex and swallowing, tests the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves together. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"What mobility characteristics should be assessed for a breast mass, and what accompanying finding is noted?","choices":["Only mobility relative to the chest wall, since skin mobility is not a reliable clinical indicator","Mobility in relation to the skin and to the chest wall, noting any accompanying dimpling","Mobility is not a relevant characteristic for breast masses; only size and consistency are assessed","Only whether the mass is tender to palpation, without noting mobility characteristics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Mass mobility relative to both the skin and the chest wall is assessed, along with noting any accompanying dimpling. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"During the bimanual exam, what is assessed when palpating the cervix and fornices for movement?","choices":["Any movement at all is abnormal","Movement is not assessed during the bimanual exam","Normal movement is 1-2cm without pain","Only pain is assessed, not the degree of movement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cervical/fornix movement of 1-2cm without pain is the normal finding on bimanual exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which of the following belongs in the plan rather than anywhere else in the note?","choices":["The chronic conditions that also require treatment","A general assessment or overall impression of the patient","The seven attributes of the presenting symptom itself","Follow-up instructions, and what should bring the patient back sooner"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The plan covers disposition, testing, treatment, referrals, patient education and follow-up. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What proportion of the breast lies superficial to the pectoral muscle versus the serratus anterior?","choices":["Entirely superficial to the serratus anterior only","Entirely superficial to the pectoral muscle only","1/3 superficial to the pectoral muscle, 2/3 superficial to the serratus anterior","2/3 superficial to the pectoral muscle, 1/3 superficial to the serratus anterior"],"answer":3,"exp":"The breast is 2/3 superficial to the pectoral muscle and 1/3 superficial to the serratus anterior. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Where are the subscapular (posterior) axillary nodes located?","choices":["At the lower border of pectoralis major, inside the axillary fold","Along the lateral border of the scapula, deep in the posterior axillary fold","High in the apex of the axilla, close to the axillary vein","Along the upper part of the humerus, near the axillary vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Subscapular (posterior) nodes are located along the lateral scapular border, deep in the posterior axillary fold. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which question appears on the lecture's checklist for reviewing a completed note?","choices":["Is the tone professional and does the note convey neutrality?","Has the note been countersigned by a second clinician?","Does the note fit within a single printed page?","Have all abbreviations been expanded in a glossary?"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The checklist asks about organisation, whether the picture is clear, whether pertinent positives and negatives are specifically described, whether anything is missing, whether it is too wordy, and about professional neutral tone. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How does the lecture say family history should be structured?","choices":["Only first-degree relatives, listed alphabetically by name","Only relatives with a confirmed genetic diagnosis","Three generations, listed oldest to youngest, with age and health status","Two generations, listed youngest to oldest, with cause of death"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture specifies three generations oldest to youngest, with age, living or deceased status, health status and diseases, to identify possible genetic patterns. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why is the patient's head and shoulders elevated during the pelvic exam?","choices":["It improves the examiner's line of sight to the cervix","It helps relax the abdominal muscles","It reduces the risk of vasovagal syncope","It is required for speculum insertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Elevating the head and shoulders helps relax the abdominal musculature, aiding the exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"Muscle bulk is assessed by doing what?","choices":["Assessing resistance to passive stretch","Inspecting the size and contour of the muscles","Grading strength against examiner resistance","Striking the tendon with a reflex hammer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Muscle bulk is assessed by inspecting the size and contour of the muscles, noting whether they are flat, concave, or atrophied, especially in the hands, shoulders, and thighs. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"In what order does the lecture say an oral presentation should run?","choices":["Beginning with the assessment, so the listener knows the conclusion first","In the order the information was obtained during the encounter","Beginning with the physical examination findings","The same order as written reports"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That order runs from chief complaint and history of present illness through to assessment and plan. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"During the rectovaginal exam, what should the patient be asked to do as the middle finger enters the anus?","choices":["Hold their breath","Cough forcefully","Fully relax with no active maneuver","Bear down as if defecating"],"answer":3,"exp":"Asking the patient to bear down as if defecating during anal finger insertion eases entry and relaxes the sphincter. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is assessed regarding breast size and symmetry on inspection?","choices":["Perfect symmetry between the breasts is expected in every healthy, unaffected patient","Only size is formally assessed on inspection, since symmetry is highly variable","Size and symmetry are not part of the standard inspection, only palpation findings","Size and symmetry, noting that some differences are common and normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Size/symmetry are assessed, and the lecture notes that some asymmetry between breasts is common and normal. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the lecture require alongside each past medical history entry?","choices":["The name of the facility where the care was delivered","A copy of the discharge summary for every hospitalization","Specific dates and timelines","Confirmation that the patient consented to each treatment given"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture asks for specific dates and timelines across major illnesses, surgeries, hospitalizations, childhood illnesses, immunizations, and screenings. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What limitation does the lecture note about simulated or standardized patients?","choices":["Script and findings cannot exactly mimic the real patient experience","They cannot be used to practise the physical examination at all","Their histories are always internally inconsistent","They may not be used for graded assessments in the program"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names tone of voice, body language, and things left unsaid as what a script cannot reproduce. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which pearl does the lecture offer for delivering a presentation?","choices":["Present the findings in whatever order they happen to come to mind","Tell a story, and try to make it interesting","Include every detail, since listeners cannot ask questions afterwards","Read directly from your notes so that nothing at all is omitted"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pearls are to stay relaxed, keep order, avoid reading notes, tell an interesting story, and err on saying less. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"The biceps deep tendon reflex tests primarily which nerve roots?","choices":["L2 and L3","S1 and S2","C5 and C6","T10 and T11"],"answer":2,"exp":"The biceps deep tendon reflex tests primarily the C5 and C6 nerve roots. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the sitting position used for during the breast exam?","choices":["Inspection — it exaggerates dimpling or retractions","Palpation only","Only for the axillary lymph node exam, not general inspection","Bimanual pelvic-style palpation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sitting position is used for inspection because it exaggerates dimpling or retraction findings. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which of the following is a landmark palpated at the knee joint?","choices":["The greater trochanter","The acromion process","The tibial tuberosity","The medial malleolus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Knee palpation includes the patella and patellar tendon, the medial and lateral femoral condyles, the tibial tuberosity, the tibial plateaus, and the joint lines. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"On the 0-to-5 muscle strength scale, what does a grade of 5 indicate?","choices":["Full range of motion against gravity with full resistance","Full range of motion, but not against any gravity","Only a trace of movement","No evidence of movement at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"A muscle strength grade of 5 is full range of motion against gravity with full resistance — normal strength. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"How are the Bartholin's glands palpated?","choices":["Insert two fingers fully into the vagina and sweep them laterally across the wall from the introitus to the fornices, checking for masses","Insert the index finger into the vagina at the posterior introitus, keeping the thumb external, and palpate between thumb and index finger at the 5 and 7 o'clock positions","Palpate externally only, pressing along the labia majora and perineal skin, without ever inserting a finger into the vagina at all","Use the open speculum to visualize the gland openings directly and press externally on them without any digital palpation of the tissue"],"answer":1,"exp":"This bidigital technique (internal index finger, external thumb) at the 5 and 7 o'clock positions is used to palpate the Bartholin's glands. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"Ankle plantar flexion by the gastrocnemius and soleus is primarily innervated by which nerve root?","choices":["C7 (radial nerve)","L2 (femoral nerve)","C5 (axillary nerve)","S1 (tibial nerve)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ankle plantar flexion by the gastrocnemius and soleus is primarily innervated by the S1 nerve root through the tibial nerve. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"On inspection of the posterior neck, the spinous process is most prominent at which level?","choices":["The C1 and C2 level","The mid-thoracic T6 level","The lumbosacral L5 level","The C7 and T1 level"],"answer":3,"exp":"The spinous process is most prominent at the C7 and T1 level and serves as a surface landmark when inspecting the posterior spine. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"When stroking the sole of the foot, the normal plantar (Babinski) response in an adult is what?","choices":["Upward extension of the great toe","Downward flexion of the toes","No movement of the foot at all","Rhythmic clonus at the ankle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The normal plantar response in an adult is downward flexion of the toes; an upgoing great toe is abnormal and reflects a corticospinal tract lesion. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"A patient oriented to person, place, and time is documented as oriented to what degree?","choices":["Oriented times one","Oriented times four","Oriented times five","Oriented times three"],"answer":3,"exp":"A patient oriented to person, place, and time is documented as oriented times three; adding event makes it times four. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why should the patient empty her bladder before the pelvic exam?","choices":["It prevents contamination of the pelvic exam equipment and sterile instrument tray","A full bladder compromises the bimanual exam, impedes palpation of organs, and creates discomfort","It has no real clinical effect on exam quality, and emptying the bladder is only a courtesy","It is only necessary if a Pap smear and endocervical sample will be performed"],"answer":1,"exp":"An empty bladder is important because a full bladder compromises the bimanual exam, impedes organ palpation, and causes patient discomfort. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Besides explaining why each possibility was considered, what else may the differential include, according to the lecture?","choices":["The tests that would help rule it in or out","The likely length of any hospital stay for each","The likely cost of treating each possibility","The patient's own stated preference among them"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture allows the differential to name those tests. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"During dynamic inspection, what should the examiner observe for while the patient raises her arms and presses her hands against her hips?","choices":["Skin tethering, retraction, dimpling, and asymmetry of movement","Only the presence or character of nipple discharge","Only the size and firmness of the axillary lymph nodes","Only the skin temperature over the breast surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are the specific findings to observe for during dynamic inspection maneuvers. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"The hypoglossal nerve (cranial nerve twelve) is tested by asking the patient to do what?","choices":["Protrude the tongue and move it side to side","Shrug the shoulders upward strongly","Follow a finger through the cardinal gazes","Identify a familiar odor with eyes closed"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hypoglossal nerve, which innervates the intrinsic tongue muscles, is tested by having the patient protrude the tongue and move it side to side, watching for deviation, atrophy, or fasciculations. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the purpose of offering a mirror during the pelvic exam?","choices":["It is required for accurate cervical inspection","It is used to demonstrate speculum insertion technique","It substitutes for the room's overhead lighting","It allows the woman to view the exam, helping her relax"],"answer":3,"exp":"Offering a mirror lets the patient view the exam, which can help her relax. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Normal elbow extension is closest to which value?","choices":["About 0 to negative 5 degrees","About 90 degrees of remaining flexion","About 160 degrees","About 45 degrees"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal elbow extension is about 0 to negative 5 degrees — the arm straightens fully or slightly past straight. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"On external inspection, what findings should be noted regarding the mons pubis and labia majora?","choices":["Only the color and coarseness of the pubic hair","Only the skin temperature over the mons and labia","Inflammation, ulcerations, discharge, nodules, and vesicles","Only whether a chaperone is present in the room"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific findings to note when inspecting the mons pubis and labia majora. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"Cranial nerve two, the optic nerve, is tested by which of the following?","choices":["Testing the sense of smell","Palpating the masseter muscle","Having the patient shrug the shoulders","Testing visual acuity with an eye chart"],"answer":3,"exp":"The optic nerve is tested through visual acuity with an eye chart, inspection of the optic fundi, visual fields by confrontation, and the pupillary light reflex. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"Where does adipose tissue predominate in the breast?","choices":["Both superficially and deeply, surrounding the glandular tissue","Only directly beneath the nipple and areola","Adipose tissue is not a significant component of breast composition","Only in the deepest layer, adjacent to the chest wall"],"answer":0,"exp":"Adipose tissue surrounds the glandular tissue and predominates both superficially and deeply. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A colleague reads your differential and cannot tell why you favoured one diagnosis over the others. Which standard from the lecture has the note failed?","choices":["That the differential be limited to a single leading diagnosis","That the differential be written in the patient's own words","That other providers be able to understand your thought process from it","That every possible diagnosis for that organ be listed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this directly as the purpose of the differential statements. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What nipple finding is described as a possible normal variant, versus one that is concerning for cancer?","choices":["Inversion can be a normal variant; retraction is concerning for cancer","Both inversion and retraction always indicate an underlying malignancy requiring biopsy","Retraction is always a normal variant; inversion is always concerning for cancer","Both inversion and retraction are always normal, benign findings regardless of onset"],"answer":0,"exp":"Nipple inversion can be a normal variant, while retraction (a change to a previously normal nipple) is concerning for cancer. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"On what does the lecture say the assessment is based?","choices":["The chief complaint alone, since it defines the visit","The patient's stated preference among the possible diagnoses","The review of systems, which surveys every body system","The history, the physical examination, and diagnostic testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names all three, and notes the assessment uses the same format as the history and physical assessment. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"On the muscle strength scale, a grade of 3 corresponds to what?","choices":["No evidence of any muscle movement","Full range of motion against gravity but not against resistance","Full muscle strength against complete applied resistance","Movement only with gravity eliminated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Grade 3 muscle strength is full range of motion against gravity but not against added resistance. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which details does the lecture require for a pharmacologic entry in the plan?","choices":["Indications, drug name, dosage, route, and frequency","Drug class, an alternative agent, and the expected cost","Prescription number, dispensing pharmacy, and refill count","Drug name only, since dosing is set by the pharmacist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists all five for the pharmacologic component of treatment. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the structure of a patient interview?","choices":["Structure matters, but be flexible and adapt to the patient's needs","A fixed sequence should be followed identically for every patient","Structure should be abandoned entirely in favour of free conversation","The patient should be given a written form to complete first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states there is no perfect formula for every patient, that structure is important but not everything, and that the clinician should adapt. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A muscle that moves through full range of motion only when gravity is eliminated is graded as what?","choices":["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 4","Grade 5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Grade 2 muscle strength is full range of motion but not against gravity — movement only when gravity is eliminated. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Bringing the fingers toward the midline with the palms down tests which wrist movement?","choices":["Wrist hyperextension","Radial deviation","Ulnar deviation","Finger abduction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bringing the fingers toward the midline with palms down is radial deviation, normally about 20 to 25 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which technique does the lecture describe as repeating a patient's own words back to them?","choices":["Echoing","Signposting, which announces that the interview is moving to a different section","Validation, which conveys that the patient's emotional response is understandable","Summarization, which condenses the account so far and invites correction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists echoing alongside clarification under guided questioning as a way to invite the patient to continue in their own terms. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How is the technique for separating the labia to expose internal structures performed?","choices":["Using two cotton swabs held gently against the labial folds","Using the index finger and thumb to spread the labia minora laterally","Using a speculum blade to spread the tissue before manual inspection","This step is unnecessary whenever a speculum will later be used"],"answer":1,"exp":"The labia minora are spread laterally using the index finger and thumb to expose the clitoris, urethral meatus, and introitus. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 25"}]};
